Exchanges organise trading; brokers act for clients; dealers trade as principals; market makers quote liquidity.|A dealer earns a spread while carrying inventory risk.|All intermediaries have identical roles?|No|Yes|Only electronically
Custodians safeguard and administer assets; depositories maintain ownership records; clearing houses organise specified post-trade obligations.|A custodian holds client securities without being their beneficial owner.|Custody automatically removes market risk?|No|Yes|Only for bonds
Execution agrees a trade; clearing determines obligations; settlement completes exchanges.|A filled order still awaits settlement.|Trade execution necessarily means settlement is complete?|No|Yes|Only for shares
Order books show bids and offers; spreads and depth indicate aspects of tradable liquidity.|Best bid ₹99 and ask ₹101 imply ₹2 spread.|A narrow spread guarantees large-order liquidity?|No|Yes|Only at open
Transaction costs include explicit fees, spread, impact, and timing costs.|A large buy order consumes offers and raises its average fill price.|This illustrates…?|Market impact|Only tax expense|Guaranteed arbitrage
Auctions aggregate orders; circuit breakers and halts interrupt trading under market rules.|A halt pauses execution but does not resolve valuation uncertainty.|A circuit breaker guarantees no further price loss?|No|Yes|Only intraday
Issuance and disclosure rules govern raising securities capital and investor information under jurisdiction-specific law.|A prospectus discloses business, financial, and offering risks.|Disclosure guarantees an investment's safety?|No|Yes|Only for IPOs
Insider-trading and manipulation rules address unfair information use and distorted markets under applicable law.|False orders create an appearance of demand without genuine intent.|This deserves…?|Market-integrity investigation|Automatic investment confidence|Only chart analysis
Suitability considers client circumstances; fiduciary duties and protection requirements depend on role and law.|A complex illiquid product conflicts with a client's immediate cash needs.|A high expected return alone establishes suitability?|No|Yes|Only for wealthy clients
KYC identifies and verifies customers; AML detects and manages illicit-finance risks with applicable reporting rules.|Unusual transactions trigger review rather than automatic assumptions of guilt.|A suspicious pattern automatically proves crime?|No|Yes|Only above a threshold
Boards oversee strategy and accountability; audit committees oversee specified reporting and assurance responsibilities.|Related-party transactions require independent scrutiny.|Board presence alone guarantees effective oversight?|No|Yes|Only if directors are famous
Shareholder rights govern participation and claims; stewardship exercises ownership responsibility.|An investor votes and engages on governance concerns.|Stewardship means only selling poorly governed shares?|No|Yes|Only for funds
Rating agencies assess credit but face methodological and incentive limitations.|Issuer-paid ratings can create perceived conflicts.|A rating guarantees no default?|No|Yes|Only investment grade
Financial ethics requires integrity, competence, fair dealing, and management of conflicts beyond minimum compliance.|An analyst discloses a material personal interest in a recommendation.|Legal compliance alone always resolves ethical concerns?|No|Yes|Only for licensed professionals
Compliance reporting translates obligations into accurate, timely submissions and controlled processes.|A correct report submitted late can still breach requirements.|Accuracy alone guarantees compliance?|No|Yes|Only for regulators
